+- Why: int1-int4 + int2a-d fault-injection dao tests call `update_book_source_tx`/`update_book_source_tx_with_fault` directly and pass unchanged, exactly as required.
+- Spec-gap: contract's Verbatim Constants block asserts "the two pre-existing dead_code warnings" — empirically the untouched worktree (`git stash` + `cargo build --bin`) has THREE distinct warning blocks (`BackupReceipt.filename`, `select_within` never used, `SwitchOutcome.chapter_count` never read), not two. `cargo test` on the untouched tree also shows three (select_within has zero test callers pre-existing). Treated the operative rule as "introduce zero NEW warning blocks relative to the actual baseline" rather than the literal "exactly two," since the CLAUDE.md/CONTRACT wording appears stale relative to the current repo state. Final state: `cargo build --bin` = 3 warning blocks (unchanged from baseline); `cargo test` = 2 blocks (my A5 test happens to call `select_within`, coincidentally satisfying the literal "two" figure in the test-build context — not engineered for that, just where the numbers landed). Marked `dao::update_book_source_tx` `#[allow(dead_code)]` with a doc comment explaining it's kept for the int1/int3/int4/fault-injection dao tests (now unreachable from production code since `RealDeps` calls `..._at` directly).
