+- Spec-gap (test collision, supersede rule invoked): `req003_scenario1_three_sources_all_hit` fed the SAME book name+author to three sources and asserted three UNCOLLAPSED rows — this is now exactly C1's target case (the file's own header comment had explicitly called multi-source dedup "out of scope" at the time that test was written). Updated the assertion to expect one `FoldedHit` with 3 sources instead of reverting to the old (now-wrong) three-Hit-rows shape; logged here per the B5/C6 supersede rule. `req003_scenario2`/`req003_scenario3` used identical incidental "X" names across sources for an orthogonal reason (funnel/deadline/error-isolation testing, not folding) — gave those fixtures distinct names (`X_A`/`X_B`/`X_C`) instead of changing assertions, since folding was never the intent there and preserving the original per-source-row assertions was cleaner than reinterpreting them.
+- B/C test strategy: pure fn-level tests for the fold key (including the None/Some("") collapse-together case — C2 "depends inversely"), first-occurrence-slot preservation (C3), first-seen Enter target (C4), and a StatusLine-interleaved-between-duplicates case (C5, as literally required by the contract). Two direct `format_hit_row` tests (count==1, count>=2) plus one real-call-path test that goes through `assemble_rows` (not a hand-built enum) before calling the same helper `draw()` uses.
+
+- Final warning check: `cargo build --bin novel-looker` (production, no cfg(test)) = 3 warning blocks, identical set to the untouched baseline (`filename`, `select_within`, `SwitchOutcome` unread fields) — zero NEW warnings introduced despite Items A/B/C substantially changing all three affected files.
